  date: "2026-09-09",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 029: Mask inspection and repair stop errors in a reusable master pattern from repeatedly damaging the chips made from it.

Lesson 030: Mask writers turn digital chip designs into physical masks, so their speed affects how quickly new chips can reach production.

Lesson 031: More chip-factory output only becomes useful AI when memory, packaging, data centers and software can support the extra hardware.

Lesson 032: Advanced packaging connects separate compute and memory dies, and its area and yield affect how many working accelerators can ship.

Lesson 033: HBM provides nearby working memory, but its capacity, bandwidth and latency solve different limits on an AI processor.

The big idea

A monolithic processor places its main functions on one large die. A chiplet design divides those functions across several smaller dies and reconnects them inside one package.

This can improve manufacturing yield, let each function use a suitable process and make designs easier to reuse. But the boundaries between dies add communication, packaging, testing and software costs.

The correct comparison is therefore not one large die versus several cheap small dies. It is the performance and cost of one finished working product.

Where we are

Chip design → separate dies → test each die → connect them in an advanced package → test the complete product

1. What physically changes?

Inside one die, signals travel through microscopic metal wires built during wafer fabrication. In a chiplet product, some signals must leave one die, cross package wiring or a bonded interface, and enter another die.

The package can hold several compute dies, an input/output die, cache, memory or other specialized blocks.

That is why chiplets depend on Lesson 032. Splitting silicon only works if packaging can provide enough connections with acceptable power, latency and reliability.

2. Why smaller dies can improve manufacturing output

Recall Lesson 017: a defect is more likely to spoil a large die because the die occupies more wafer area. Smaller dies also let a manufacturer test pieces first and reserve working pieces for assembly. These are known-good dies.

Use an invented example. Ignore wafer-edge losses and assume one wafer offers 70,000 square millimeters of usable area.

A 700 mm² monolithic die gives 100 candidates. At 50% die yield, that produces 50 working dies.

Now divide the design into four 175 mm² chiplets. The wafer gives 400 candidates. At 80% yield, it produces 320 good chiplets, enough for 80 four-chiplet sets. If final package yield is 90%, 72 complete products work.

In this example, chiplets raise output from 50 to 72 products. The figures are teaching assumptions, not a universal chiplet rule. Real results depend on defect patterns, edge losses, die mix, test coverage, package yield and performance requirements.

3. Use the right process for each function

Not every circuit benefits equally from the newest manufacturing process.

Dense compute logic may justify an advanced node. Input/output circuits, analog functions or cache may be economical on another process. A chiplet architecture can combine dies made with different technologies rather than forcing every block onto the most expensive node.

TSMC describes this as heterogeneous integration. AMD's CDNA 4 design, for example, uses an advanced process for performance-sensitive compute dies while keeping some other functions on a different process.

This can reduce cost and let one block change without redesigning everything, while adding production flows and qualification steps that must arrive together.

4. The interconnect is not free

